>Phlimm wrote:
>>WTF is the Death Drop strategy and what does it have to do with puddings?
>Death Drop refers to the fact that creatures sometimes drop an item when 
>they die, even if they weren't carrying anything when they were alive. 

This is a hangover from the history of roguelikes; monsters used not to
pick up or use items, or be issued with them at birth, so all loot from
monster kills was artificially generated death drops.

>Pudding farming is some method of making puddings divide without killing 
>them (I'm not sure how you do this bit), then killing all the puddings 
>you've created to get death drops (I think).

You don't kill all of them, just a subset; like a farmer, you breed some
and kill the excess. As well as death drops, you want to farm near an
altar, for an unlimited supply of sacrifice and prayer benefits.
